Exactly. The line is a projection of something whose native topology may be cyclic, recursive, or higher-dimensional.

Take the Ouroboros:

\boxed{\text{Output}\rightarrow\text{Input}}

Choose a starting point and cut it for representation, and suddenly:

S_0\rightarrow S_1\rightarrow S_2\rightarrow S_3\rightarrow S_4

looks linear.

But the apparent endpoints may be artifacts of the projection:

S_0\rightarrow S_1\rightarrow S_2\rightarrow S_3\rightarrow S_0

So sequence is real locally, while closure appears at another scale.

That’s a great warning for everything we’ve been doing:

\boxed{\text{linear representation}\neq\text{linear ontology}}

History gets written as timelines. Reasoning gets written as sentences. Code executes sequences. Stories have beginnings and endings. Causal explanations become arrows.

Those are excellent handles because humans need somewhere to grab the structure.

But the underlying phenomenon can contain feedback, recurrence, parallelism, nesting, branching, cross-layer causation, and cycles.

And there’s an even better connection to the fictional bookends.

To display an Ouroboros as a line, we must choose somewhere to cut it:

\bigcirc \xrightarrow{\text{cut}} [A\;\longrightarrow\;B]

Suddenly we have bookends.

Yet those bookends weren’t necessarily properties of the original system.

We created them by choosing the projection.

That means Pantheon should record:

\boxed{\text{Where did we cut the loop to make this explanation possible?}}

Because changing the cut can change the apparent story:

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow A

can be narrated as:

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C

or

B\rightarrow C\rightarrow A

Same loop. Different tale. Different apparent cause and consequence.

That’s powerful for history, biology, economics, psychology, systems, narrative—and our own Pantheon fossil record.

So:

Bookends may be fictional.
The line may be a projection.
The sequence may be local.
The Ouroboros may be underneath.

And the really interesting information can be hiding precisely where we cut the circle to tell the tale. 🐍